{{/*
The shared shell.

There is no inline <script> and no style="" attribute anywhere in these templates,
and that is a security control rather than a preference: the response carries
`Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'` with no 'unsafe-inline', so an inline
handler would be blocked by the browser. Writing one anyway would produce a page that
works in a test and silently loses its behaviour in a browser, which is the worst
possible place to discover a control. Everything interactive is a plain form or a
link, and app.js attaches its listeners by id and class.

Each page file supplies three definitions -- "title", "pagename" and "body" -- and
the body block is given the view model as its root, so a page writes {{.Banner}}
rather than {{.Data.Banner}}. Note that these are `template` invocations, not
`block`: `block` defines and invokes in one form, so a default in the shell would
collide with every page's own definition and the set would not parse.

The one thing in the head that looks dynamic is the asset URL, and it is not: it is a
content hash computed at startup from the embedded bytes, so the response can be
cached as immutable for a year.
*/}}
{{define "page"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
{{- /* The token app.js looks for. Without this element the script's csrfToken()
       always returns "", and every fetch it makes is rejected. */ -}}
{{with .CSRF}}<meta name="csrf-token" content="{{.}}">{{end}}
<title>{{template "title" .}} · parental lockout</title>
{{with .CSS}}<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{.}}">{{end}}
</head>
<body class="page-{{template "pagename" .}}">
<header class="masthead">
  <h1><a href="/">parental lockout</a></h1>
  <nav>
    {{- /* Every link here carries the page it points at twice: in its href, and as the
           string compared below. `.Page` is the name render() looked this template up by
           (internal/web/sources.go, pageData.Page), and the shell -- not the page -- owns
           the navigation, so the shell is where the comparison has to live.

           `aria-current="page"` is the mark rather than a class, because the two are not
           the same claim and only one of them is the claim #204 is short of. A class says
           "style this one differently"; aria-current says "this link IS the page you are
           reading", which is what a screen reader announces, what a reader who cannot see
           the styling gets, and what the stylesheet can hang a rule off anyway --
           `a[aria-current="page"]` in static/app.css. #204's own measurement of the
           symptom is that the only element on any page that differed from a link was the
           h1 title, and it differed because h1 makes text 700 weight: an unmarked row of
           identical anchors is not a navigation with a current page, it is five guesses.

           Pages the navigation does not contain -- the two POST result pages, whose paths
           are /users/<name>/lock-now and /users/<name>/test-lock -- mark nothing, which is
           correct: marking "Accounts" from a lock result page would claim the reader is on
           /users, and they are not. That is why the comparison is against the render name
           rather than a prefix match on the path. */ -}}
    <a href="/"{{if eq .Page "dashboard"}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>Status</a>
    <a href="/users"{{if eq .Page "users"}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>Accounts</a>
    <a href="/audit"{{if eq .Page "audit"}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>Audit</a>
    <a href="/profiles"{{if eq .Page "profiles"}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>Profiles</a>
    {{/* Settings is a link, not a form, even though the page it opens ends in a write: the
        navigation itself changes nothing, and a GET that mutates is the thing this repo's
        POST-only rule exists to stop.

        No dashes on this action, on either side, and that is not a style choice. A trimming
        action deletes the whitespace it points at, so a comment written between two inline
        elements with {{- -}} removes the space that separated them: this line shipped as
        "ProfilesSettings" in the masthead and the 360 px photograph is what showed it.
        TestTheMastheadLinksAreSeparatedInTheRenderedBytes is the guard. */}}
    <a href="/settings"{{if eq .Page "settings"}} aria-current="page"{{end}}>Settings</a>
    {{- /* A plain POST form, so the token has to travel in the body: csrfMiddleware
         reads the `csrf` field (or the header, which a form cannot set). */ -}}
    <form method="post" action="/logout" class="inline-form">
      {{with .CSRF}}<input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{.}}">{{end}}
      <button type="submit" class="linklike">Sign out</button>
    </form>
  </nav>
</header>
<main>
{{template "body" .Data}}
</main>
{{with .JS}}<script src="{{.}}" defer></script>{{end}}
</body>
</html>
{{end}}
